<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Portoferraio</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cosmopoli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Portoferraio is the main town and port of the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a>, off the coast of Tuscany. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici">Cosimo I de' Medici</a> founded it in 1548 as a fortified port named Cosmopoli, with bastions designed to guard the Tuscan coast against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> lived there during his exile on Elba in 1814–1815, before his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/portoferraio.jpg" alt="Portoferraio" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carsten Steger · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_image_of_Portoferraio_(view_from_the_northwest).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On Elba Cosimo founded the fortified port of <b>Portoferraio</b>, originally named Cosmopoli, in 1548, and Livorno was enclosed by modern bastions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>HMS Undaunted brought the deposed emperor to <b>Portoferraio</b>, where he landed the next morning to rule his tiny island principality.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-3">3 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its small size made it dependent on stronger powers: Cosimo I de' Medici built <b>Portoferraio</b> on Elba in 1548, and Spain later held Porto Longone.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-piombino">Principality of Piombino</a></li></ul></section>
